Master Linux for DevOps: Commands You Must Know 🌐

Linux is the backbone of modern DevOps. Here’s a comprehensive cheat sheet for mastering Linux commands:

1️⃣ File and Directory Management
🔹 ls, cd, pwd – Navigate directories
🔹 mkdir, rmdir, touch – Create folders/files
🔹 cp, mv, rm – Copy, move, delete files
🔹 find, locate – Search files

2️⃣ File Permissions and Ownership
🔸 chmod – Change permissions
🔸 chown, chgrp – Change ownership
🔸 umask – Default permission settings

3️⃣ Process Management
🔹 ps, top, htop – Monitor processes
🔹 kill, pkill, killall – Terminate processes
🔹 bg, fg, jobs – Manage background/.     foreground jobs

4️⃣ Networking
🔸 ping, curl, wget – Test connectivity, fetch     content
🔸 netstat, ss – Network stats and connections
🔸 scp, rsync – Secure file transfer

5️⃣ Disk and System Monitoring
🔹 df, du – Disk usage
🔹 free, vmstat – Memory usage
🔹 uptime, dmesg – System uptime/logs

6️⃣ User and Group Management
🔸 useradd, usermod, passwd – Add/modify users
🔸 groups, id – View user groups
🔸 su, sudo – Switch users/privileges

7️⃣ Automation and Scripting
🔹 crontab, at – Schedule tasks
🔹 bash – Run scripts
🔹 alias – Create custom shortcuts
